{
  "revised_script": {
    "cold_open": "The old library stood silent, its shelves heavy with forgotten stories. Dust danced in the light, as if time itself hesitated to move forward.",
    "question": "Why did the same book return every ten years, always by the same woman, unchanged?",
    "journey": "Each decade, the woman returned, her face untouched by time. The librarian, now older, watched her with quiet wonder. The book, never titled, held no name, only a single blank page. Over the years, the librarian began to notice small changes: a flower pressed between the pages, a faded letter, a date—always ten years apart. The woman never spoke, only smiled, as if she carried a secret too vast for words.",
    "discovery": "One day, the librarian found a letter inside the book, written in the woman's hand. It read, 'I return this book to keep the story alive, for the next reader who will find it in ten years.' The librarian realized the woman was not just a visitor—she was the story itself, returning to ensure its continuation.",
    "emotional_turn": "The librarian felt a deep ache, not of loss, but of connection. The woman had been watching, waiting, and holding the story together across time.",
    "payoff": "When the woman returned again, the librarian handed her the book with a quiet nod. This time, the woman left a new letter inside, for the next decade, and a single flower pressed between the pages.",
    "after_image": "The library stood silent, the book waiting for its next return."
  },
